## `-visual` is not a neutral flag (measured 19.09, cycle `T1`)

The plant harness has TWO deterministic runs per switch set, and `-visual` chooses which: the
recorded trajectory diverges at row 2 (t = 0.01 s) of 1600 — the apple in 1599 rows (1.725 N·m in
`J00_springTorque` at that row, still 7.7e-04 deg apart at the last row because J08 never leaves
its soft stop) and the oak in 1231 rows (5.95e-03 deg, but it CONVERGES back to a 0.000e+00
last-row delta because it ends pinned on its trunk stop). It is the harness and not a regression:
on the OLD player the `-visual` apple label `apple_t1c2_photo_hold` is bit-identical to today's
`-visual` label `apple_t1d_hold`, and the `-visual`-free runs are bit-identical to the accepted
`apple_t1sw_*`/`apple_t1c2_*` pair, so both variants reproduce across a full rebuild.

- **Compare labels only within the same switch shape.** A physics label must NOT carry `-visual`
  (the apple canon is `apple_t1sw_*`/`apple_t1c2_*`/`apple_t1d_nv_*`); a picture label
  (`apple_t1c2_photo_*`, `apple_t1d_*`) must never be quoted as the canon, and a number taken
  from one shape is not evidence about the other.
- The oak's labels all carry `-visual` because `tree-probe.sh` always passes it. That is
  consistent, and today's oak pair is bit-identical to the accepted `tree_t1oak_*`, so the oak
  canon is sound — but a future oak run made WITHOUT `-visual` is a different trajectory.

